on locations — ABB Limited (UK), ABB Engineering (Shanghai) Ltd. (China), ABB Inc. (USA), ABB India Limited (India). These are production locations, i.e., manufacturers. The schema has manufacturers[]. Include them.\n\nRevision history:\n- revision \"Initial\", date 2020-08-14, changes \"First issue.\"\n- \"1\", 2020-09-30, \"Additional evaluation of reduced bore larger sizes up to DN600.\"\n- \"2\", 2020-12-24, \"Additional evaluation of (virtual) full bore sensor and additional software version\"\n- \"3\", 2021-08-02, \"Additional evaluation for extending temperature to – 25 °C and for E2 class and O class for the flow transmitter. Addition of software version.\"\n- \"4\", 2021-08-26, \"Addition of full-bore sizes up to DN1200.\"\n- \"5\", 2021-11-23, \"Addition of full-bore sizes up to DN1800.\"\n- \"6\", 2022-05-20, \"Addition of accuracy class 1 to DN1800.\"\n- \"7\", 2022-08-30, \"Software version updates due to addition of microprocessors.\"\n\nDates format: use ISO? The schema doesn't specify; I'll use ISO dates \"2020-08-14\".\n\nRecommendation: R49. The number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" — recommendation R49, edition? The 2013 refers to... Actually in NMi numbering \"R49/2013\" might be recommendation R 49 (2013 edition?). Hmm, R 49 editions: 2006 (Ed. 2006), amendments 2012/2013. R 49-1:2013? The number format R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01: \"R49/2013\" — could mean edition 2013. The scheme is A. accuracy_classes: mention of accuracy class 1, E2 class, O class in revision history. R49 accuracy classes are numeric: 1, 2. Class \"E2\" and \"O\" are temperature classes (OIML D... actually for water meters, temperature classes like O, M, T, U per R49: O: 5-30°C, M: 5-55°C, T: 5-70°C, U: 0-70°C; humidity classes B, O, etc. Actually R49 uses temperature class (O, M, T, U) and environment class B, O, C). E2... hmm, \"E2 class\" — in R49 there are accuracy classes 1 and 2, and metrological classes... Actually E1, E2 are environmental classes per R49 (electromagnetic environment classes E1, E2). And O is a temperature class. So accuracy_classes: [1]. Should I include recommendation with accuracy_classes [1]? The revision history says \"Addition of accuracy class 1 to DN1800\" — accuracy class 1 present. Maybe also class 2 but not stated in this fragment. I'll include accuracy_classes: [1] since it's mentioned. Hmm, but is it safe? The cert probably covers both class 1 and 2, but fragment only shows class 1. Rule: don't fabricate. Include [1].\n\nRecommendation edition: R 49 edition — the \"2013\" in the number likely refers to the edition year (R49-1:2013, R49-2:2013, R49-3:2013 exist). So edition: 2013? The schema says edition (int). 2013 is an int. I'll use edition: 2013. Hmm, risky but plausible. Alternatively omit edition. The canonical ID: \"R49\". Scheme: \"A\".\n\nShould I include certificate.scheme: \"A\"? Yes, from \"-A-NL1-\".\n\nStructure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"project_number\": \"3528374\",\n    \"page_total\": 12,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2022-08-30\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"NMi\"\n  },\n  \"manufacturers\": [...],\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": null,\n    \"models\": [ {\"model_id\": \"DN40\"}, ... {\"model_id\": \"DN1650\"} ]\n  },\n  \"matrix_tables\": [...],\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"model_level\": [...]\n  },\n  \"recommendation\": {\n    \"id\": \"R49\",\n    \"edition\": 2013,\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"accuracy_classes\": [1]\n  },\n  \"revision_history\": [...]\n}\n```\n\nproject_number: 3528374 — number, could be int or string. I'll use string \"3528374\" or int 3528374. Schema doesn't specify; int is fine but strings safer for numbers with dots. \"3528374\" is clean; I'll use it as string? Hmm. The example in rules for certificate.number is a string. Project number — I